<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/primdetr/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/primdetr/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/primdetr/>;
    dc:modified '2013-07-11T09:54:22'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'primary_deciduous_trees'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A primary forest is a naturally regenerated forest of native species, where there are no clearly visible indications of human activities and the ecological processes are not significantly disturbed. Reference: \'Global Forest Resources Assessment: Terms and Definitions\', Forestry Department of Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations, Rome 2010, www.fao.org/forestry/14241-0d7b74f45b0d2cfef31599cc17e4c28cd.pdf. Deciduous trees lose their leaves seasonally, for example, during winter in high latitudes or following seasonal variations in rainfall.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P29::primdetr'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/primevtr/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/primevtr/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/primevtr/>;
    dc:modified '2013-07-11T09:54:22'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'primary_evergreen_trees'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A primary forest is a naturally regenerated forest of native species, where there are no clearly visible indications of human activities and the ecological processes are not significantly disturbed. Reference: \'Global Forest Resources Assessment: Terms and Definitions\', Forestry Department of Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations, Rome 2010, www.fao.org/forestry/14241-0d7b74f45b0d2cfef31599cc17e4c28cd.pdf. Evergreen trees have leaves in all seasons.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P29::primevtr'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/secodetr/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/secodetr/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/secodetr/>;
    dc:modified '2013-07-11T09:54:22'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'secondary_deciduous_trees'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A secondary forest is a forest that has been logged and has recovered naturally or artificially. Reference: \'Report of the ad hoc technical expert group on forest biological diversity\', United Nations Convention on Biological Diversity, www.cbd.int/forest/definitions.shtml. Deciduous trees lose their leaves seasonally, for example, during winter in high latitudes or following seasonal variations in rainfall.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P29::secodetr'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/secoevtr/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/secoevtr/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P29/current/secoevtr/>;
    dc:modified '2013-07-11T09:54:22'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'secondary_evergreen_trees'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A secondary forest is a forest that has been logged and has recovered naturally or artificially. Reference: \'Report of the ad hoc technical expert group on forest biological diversity\', United Nations Convention on Biological Diversity, www.cbd.int/forest/definitions.shtml. Evergreen trees have leaves in all seasons.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P29::secoevtr'^^xsd:string .
